(project pricklypear-film-edit
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: film editing — studio library (characters/assets), Nopales-authored EDLs, Blender VSE as headless assembly backend, render caching, gen-API bridge as future edge")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section film-edit

  (doc "Programmatic video editing: the image holds the studio library (characters, locations, props, style locks) and the project graph (shots, cast bindings, takes, EDLs). Blender does assembly and render headlessly; media lives on disk under content hashes. Nopales state is authoritative; .blend files and renders are derived artifacts.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (build "dune build image")
   (smoke "(load-library \"edit\") installs edit/… bindings and routes")
   (smoke "define assets/characters, a project, shots with cast, choose takes, emit an EDL")
   (smoke "GET /studio lists projects; /studio/project?slug=<slug> shows cast, shot table, and render state")
   (smoke "pp_eval edit/… entry points work for agents"))

  (details
   (doc (|
        =========================================================
        PRODUCT INTENT
        =========================================================
        A REPL-driven video editing and assembly pipeline with
        pricklypear as the brain and Blender as the muscle.

        The motivating observation: an edit is DATA — an ordered
        list of decisions over takes with in/out points — and every
        serious editing tool already treats it that way internally
        (the EDL is the oldest data structure in film). What no
        tool gives you: the decision list as queryable, branchable,
        agent-editable rows on a live image, with the GUI reduced
        to a view.

        The pipeline (each stage is data in, artifact out):
          bible (characters/locations/style) → lock canonical refs
            → script → shots + cast/ref bindings
            → expand prompt+refs from library
            → raw footage (gen API or camera) → N takes per shot
            → pick a take → lines → dialogue takes (TTS or mic)
            → cues (beds + spot SFX) → assemble (EDL) + sound bounce
            → render → review → re-cut / regenerate single shots
            → optional promote take → library asset

        Every decision is a row: asset locks, cast bindings, prompt,
        seed, model, backend, cost, rejected takes, chosen take,
        input snapshots on takes. The whole history is inspectable,
        forkable, promotable — fork a branch per cut (cast bible
        forks with it).

        Division of labor (settled design, do not relitigate):
          - PRICKLYPEAR  — brain: studio library, project graph,
            provenance, cost ledger, branching, orchestration
            vocabulary, prompt/ref expansion, EDL generation,
            render-cache keys.
          - BLENDER      — muscle: assembly (VSE), compositing,
            authored animation (deferred, see below), final render.
            Headless (blender -b) for jobs; a live GUI session is a
            VIEW onto the same scene state, never the authority.
          - GEN APIS     — rented GPUs over HTTP: video/image gen,
            premium TTS. Local ComfyUI is a LATER backend behind
            the same interface, gated on hardware.
          - FFMPEG/DAW   — sound muscle: bounce and mix are derived
            artifacts of the line/cue graph. An Ardour/Reaper
            session is a VIEW with the same re-import rule as the
            Blender GUI; the session file is never adopted as truth.
          - DISK         — media files content-addressed; the image
            holds hashes + paths + provenance, NEVER blobs for
            motion or audio masters (UI thumbs may dual-point —
            see library).

        Authority rule (the invariant that keeps this coherent):
          NOPALES STATE IS AUTHORITATIVE. .blend files and renders
          are build artifacts, regenerable from rows. Hand-tweaked
          .blend truth is forbidden — it silently forks the project
          away from its provenance. If the GUI is used, its changes
          are re-imported as row edits (a documented import path),
          never adopted by copying the file back.

        Inspired by:
          - the edit decision list (Hollywood's oldest data
            structure — timecode-addressed cuts as text)
          - build systems: render cache keyed on hash of inputs;
            change nothing, cache hit
          - Blender's own architecture: a live persistent image
            driven from a console — same shape as pricklypear

        NOT a human NLE replacement (Kdenlive stays the hand-tool;
        it can open MLT/EDL exports for human touch-up). NOT a
        blob store for clips. NOT real-time collaboration. NOT
        locked to one gen vendor — backends are config, not design.

        Killer properties:
          - Render caching falls out of the object model. The EDL
            is rows; hashing the canonical print yields a cache
            key; a render is a memoized pure function of project
            state. Re-cut one shot, re-render only what changed.
          - Continuity is a library problem, not a prompt-paste
            problem. A character is a stable id with a locked
            appearance bundle; shots bind to ids; gen expands
            bindings. Fix Mara once, re-gen only shots that cast
            her.

        =========================================================
        LAYERING
        =========================================================
        - libs/edit/lib.pp        — package edit/… (seed on system/main)
        - data_rows types         — asset, project, shot, shot-cast,
                                    shot-ref, take, edl-entry,
                                    render-job, line, cue, mix-job
        - Routes via (route …)    — /studio and project/report paths
        - services/blender_bridge — OCaml daemon: typed commands in,
          bpy socket calls out; owns the render job queue
        - blender/bridge_addon.py — thin socket-eval addon inside
          Blender; PYTHON IS FIRMWARE, written once, checked in
        - scripts/render_worker   — headless blender -b invocation
        - New kernel prims        — http (client), spawn
          (subprocess), file-stat/read-bytes-range. Additive,
          general-purpose; specified in their own subsection below
          because they are kernel surface, not package code.

        v1 vs later, honestly split:
          v1 (no new prims): studio library + cast/ref bindings,
            project graph, EDL rows, EDL export to MLT XML +
            Blender scene script ON DISK, manual render, import of
            render results, promote still/clip metadata into
            assets, line/cue sound rows, cue-sheet + ffmpeg script
            export ON DISK with manual bounce, import of bounce
            results. Fully useful for bibles, human-driven assembly,
            and agent-authored structure before any gen API.
          v2 (prims land): bridge orchestration, job queue, gen-API
            submission that EXPANDS library bindings and snapshots
            inputs on each take, render cache automation, TTS
            batches, automated dailies bounce via spawn — the
            async loop.

        =========================================================
        DATA MODEL (v1)
        =========================================================
        Types (deftype on load). Timecodes integer frames, fps on
        project — same convention as film-analysis.

        ---- Studio library (stable identity) ----

        asset   ; character, location, prop, style lock, voice, …
          project         string  ; project slug, or "" studio-wide
                                  ; on this branch
          slug            string  ; "mara", "diner-ext", "theme-A"
          kind            string  ; character | location | prop |
                                  ; style | voice | sfx | score |
                                  ; plate | still | other
          summary         string
          bible           string  ; long notes / backstory / art dir
          appearance      string  ; locked positive prompt fragment
          negative        string  ; locked negative fragment
          primary_hash    string  ; canonical still/sheet/sample
          primary_path    string  ; relative to media root
          ref_hashes      string  ; JSON list of extra content hashes
                                  ; (turnarounds, costume variants)
          voice_ref       string  ; voice id or asset slug (kind=voice)
          lora_ref        string  ; backend adapter id, or ""
          media_id        string  ; optional habitat media_blobs UUID
                                  ; for UI thumb ONLY (see boundary)
          meta            string  ; JSON bag (non-query essentials)

        A character is an asset with kind=character (not a second
        identity table). Wardrobe/costume variants are assets
        (kind=prop or kind=still) bound via shot-cast.wardrobe.

        ---- Project graph ----

        project
          slug            string  ; "short-01"
          title           string
          fps             string number
          resolution      string  ; "1920x1080"
          budget_cents    string int  ; spend cap for API gen
          notes           string

        shot    ; one narrative shot in the current cut
          project         string
          ord             string int  ; position in cut
          slug            string
          summary         string  ; script beat
          prompt          string  ; ACTION/dialogue prompt only;
                                  ; appearance comes from cast/refs
          dur_target      string int  ; frames, planned

        shot-cast  ; who appears in the shot
          shot            string  ; shot id
          asset           string  ; asset id (usually kind=character)
          wardrobe        string  ; optional asset id, or ""
          role            string  ; hero | featured | background

        shot-ref   ; non-cast reference bindings (typed, not JSON bag)
          shot            string
          asset           string  ; location plate, style, prop hero…
          purpose         string  ; ip-adapter | style | depth |
                                  ; loc | prop | plate | other
          weight          string  ; backend hint, "" ok

        NOTE: an earlier sketch had shot.refs as a JSON hash list.
        That is REJECTED. Cast and refs are rows so "which shots
        use Mara?" is a closed query. Ad-hoc one-off hashes (no
        library entry yet) are allowed by first creating a minimal
        asset (kind=still) or via take-local override fields only
        at gen time — not a free-form JSON column on shot.

        take    ; one candidate clip (or still) for a shot
          shot            string  ; shot id
          n               string int  ; take number
          backend         string  ; "fal-wan" | "replicate-ltx" |
                                  ; "local-comfy" | "camera" | …
          model           string  ; model/version string
          seed            string
          cost_cents      string int  ; 0 for local/camera
          media_hash      string  ; content hash of the file on disk
          media_path      string  ; path RELATIVE to a media root
          state           string  ; pending | done | failed | rejected
          rating          string  ; "" | human note on review
          input_snapshot  string  ; canonical JSON of EXPANDED gen
                                  ; inputs at submit (prompt, asset
                                  ; ids, appearance fragments, ref
                                  ; hashes, model). Immutable after
                                  ; land. Reproducibility + cache.
          source_assets   string  ; JSON list of asset ids used

        edl-entry  ; the cut itself — one row per timeline segment
          project         string
          cut             string  ; cut label (branch-correlated)
          ord             string int
          take            string  ; take id
          tc_in           string int  ; source in-point, frames
          dur             string int
          transition      string  ; "cut" | "dissolve:N" | "fade:N"
          audio           string  ; take id of TTS/dialogue, or ""

        render-job
          project         string
          cut             string
          edl_hash        string  ; cache key: hash of canonical EDL
          state           string  ; queued | running | done | failed
          out_hash        string  ; result media hash
          out_path        string
          log             string

        ---- Sound (production audio graph) ----

        line    ; one spoken line of dialogue
          project         string
          shot            string  ; shot id the line belongs to
          ord             string int
          character       string  ; asset id (voice via voice_ref)
          text            string
          notes           string  ; performance: "whisper, rushed"
          take            string  ; chosen audio take id, or ""

        Audio takes are ordinary take rows on the same shot with
        backend "tts-*" (premium or local) or "mic" (recorded).
        line.take points at the chosen one. ADR here means only:
        a replacement read is a new take + choose; the history of
        reads is the take list. No special ADR table.

        cue     ; continuous or spot sound on the TIMELINE
          project         string
          cut             string  ; cut label (same as edl-entry)
          ord             string int
          mode            string  ; bed | hit
          asset           string  ; kind=ambience | score | sfx
          tc_start        string int  ; timeline frames
          dur             string int  ; hit: 0 means asset length
          gain_db         string number
          loop            string  ; "1" to tile a short bed

        mix-job  ; bounce as memoized function of the audio graph
          project         string
          cut             string
          cue_hash        string  ; hash of canonical print of cues
                                  ; + chosen line takes + profile
          profile         string  ; named preset, e.g. "dailies"
          state           string  ; queued | running | done | failed
          out_hash        string
          out_path        string
          log             string

        Sound obeys the same build-system rule as picture: the
        bounce key is a hash of canonical rows; nothing changed
        means cache hit. edl-entry.audio remains a per-strip
        dialogue pointer for assembly; lines + cues are the
        production graph that feeds it.

        =========================================================
        LIBRARY RULES (characters and assets)
        =========================================================
        Three layers — do not collapse them:

          1. LIBRARY  — asset rows: stable identity + locked
             appearance/voice/ref bundle + bible text.
          2. BINDING  — shot-cast / shot-ref: usage in a shot.
          3. INSTANCE — take rows: what was actually generated or
             filmed, with input_snapshot frozen at submit.

        Expansion (used by gen-submit and by a pure preview prim):
          (edit/expand shot-id) →
            action prompt
            + each cast asset.appearance (+ wardrobe.appearance)
            + each shot-ref asset.appearance as appropriate
            + union of primary_hash/ref_hashes for ref images
            + voice_ref for TTS lanes
          That structure is what input_snapshot stores.

        Promote path (library grows from production):
          (edit/promote-take take-id kind slug) → asset row
          whose primary_hash/path copy the take's media; optional
          media_id if a UI thumb is registered. Human may then
          edit appearance/bible and re-bind shots.

        Analysis vs production (boundary with film-analysis):
          asset/character  = production bible (what we generate with)
          motif/beat       = narrative lint (what the cut means)
          Do NOT merge tables. Optional shared slug later is fine;
          v1 keeps them independent.

        Media boundary (two systems, both legitimate):
          - MOTION / masters / gen outputs: disk + content hash +
            relative path only. NEVER Postgres bytea. NEVER a VStr.
          - UI thumbs / character sheets for browser browse: MAY
            also use existing habitat media_blobs (storage.media,
            small image cap) via asset.media_id. Dual pointer is
            OK; disk hash remains authoritative for gen workers.
          - Third-party analysis corpus still never publicly
            routed — film-analysis.publishing unchanged.

        NO per-user ACL (existing habitat rule).
        NO keyframe/curve tables in v1 — authored animation waits
        for hardware (Cycles on CPU and Eevee without GPU are not
        viable); transitions are the only motion v1 authors.
        NO claim that the data model alone fixes identity drift in
        gen models — the habitat enforces provenance, forced ref
        bundles, reject/regen economics, and branch+budget. Drift
        is a model problem; reproducibility is ours.

        =========================================================
        SOUND RULES (written for people who are not sound engineers)
        =========================================================
        Audio does NOT cut with picture. Sound is layers in time:
          lines — dialogue, shot-scoped, one chosen take per line
          beds  — long spans: room tone, ambience, score region
          hits  — spot SFX pinned to a timeline frame
        That is the whole model. Everything below is habit.

        The four jobs, in the order they pay off:
          1. dialogue coverage — every line has a chosen take,
             even scratch (mic whisper or fast local TTS). A cut
             with complete scratch reads is judgeable as cinema;
             a silent one is not.
          2. presence — one ambience bed per location; cutting to
             a new room changes the bed. That alone sells space.
          3. spot SFX — doors, cloth, footsteps at cut points.
          4. mix — dialogue forward, beds well under talk, light
             limiter. Named presets, not knob archaeology.

        Dailies sound profile (the loop v2 automates):
          chosen picture strips + chosen line takes + cues
          → ffmpeg bounce with the dailies preset → proxy mp4
          with sound. Sound must NOT wait on the Blender render
          bridge; a dailies reel with scratch audio is a daily,
          a mute 4K master is not.

        Voice: character.voice_ref → kind=voice asset (sample hash
        + backend voice id). TTS reads are takes (job-shaped in
        v2, synchronous local in v1); mic reads are takes with
        backend "mic". Both are equally legitimate. Scratch first,
        hero reads later — the line id never changes.

        Music: score is cues with mode=bed over named regions.
        Temp tracks are cue rows like any other; the asset row's
        provenance fields say what may ship. Gen music is takes
        with the same provenance discipline as picture.

        NOT a DAW. NOT a sample store (wav bytes live on disk
        under hashes like motion masters). NOT auto-lip-sync: a
        line take is matched to picture by editing (trim, cutaway,
        hold), not by warping faces to audio.

        =========================================================
        THE ASYNC LOOP (job-shaped, not call-shaped)
        =========================================================
        SUBSTRATE: the jobs chapter (borge/jobs.borg) specifies
        the durable job/step/schedule machinery this vocabulary
        runs on — gen-submit is (submit-job …), gen-status is
        (job-status …) over the job row; render/mix JOBS claim
        through the same runner. The memoization-by-hash cache
        below is film-edit's own and orthogonal to job durability.

        Gen and render take minutes. The vocabulary is job-first:

          (edit/asset-upsert …)               ; library write
          (edit/shot-cast shot asset role)
          (edit/shot-ref shot asset purpose)
          (edit/expand shot-id)               ; pure preview of bundle
          (edit/gen-submit shot-id :takes 3)  ; expands + snapshots
                                              ; → job ids, returns now
          (edit/gen-status job-id)            ; pending | done | failed
          (edit/takes shot-id)                ; landed takes + costs
          (edit/choose shot-id take-n)        ; sets the cut's take
          (edit/promote-take take-id kind slug)
          (edit/line-choose line-id take-id)  ; chosen read for a line
          (edit/cue-add cut mode asset tc dur db)
          (edit/dailies cut)                  ; proxy mp4 WITH sound
          (edit/mix cut profile)              ; cue_hash → cache lookup
                                              ; → mix-job (or hit)
          (edit/render cut)                   ; EDL hash → cache lookup
                                              ; → render-job (or hit)

        A render is a memoized pure function: same EDL rows ⇒ same
        edl_hash ⇒ cache hit. Budget is a branch property: fork for
        a re-gen experiment, spend lives on the branch, main keeps
        the released cut. Cost queries are closed questions over
        take rows (spend per project/cut/backend).

        =========================================================
        BLENDER BRIDGE (protocol sketch)
        =========================================================
        services/blender_bridge (OCaml, stdlib Unix sockets):
          - accepts lines from the image over TCP (localhost only):
            (scene-clear) (strip-add <edl-entry-as-sexp>)
            (render <out-path>) (status)
          - translates to bpy calls over the addon's socket
          - owns a FIFO job queue; one render at a time
        blender/bridge_addon.py (~100 lines, checked in once):
          socket server inside Blender eval'ing a FIXED, TYPED
          command set — not arbitrary code. bpy only.
        Two modes, same protocol:
          live   — GUI open, bridge attached; the timeline is a
                   live VIEW of row edits (re-import rule applies)
          batch  — blender -b per render job, fully headless

        =========================================================
        PUBLISHING / MEDIA BOUNDARY
        =========================================================
        Own footage: fine to serve results via routes if desired.
        Third-party analysis corpus: media never enters the image,
        never gets a public route — per film-analysis.publishing.
    |)))

  (subsection model
   (doc "asset/project/shot/shot-cast/shot-ref/take/edl-entry/render-job types; content-hash media refs; cost ledger and input_snapshot on takes")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (smoke "load-library defines library + graph types with documented fields")
    (smoke "take rows carry backend/model/seed/cost/media_hash/input_snapshot")
    (smoke "edl-entry rows order a cut with tc_in/dur/transition")
    (smoke "motion masters are hashes and relative paths only — no clip bytes in Postgres")))

  (subsection library
   (doc "Studio library: asset rows (character/location/prop/style/voice/…), shot-cast and shot-ref bindings, expand + promote-take; dual media pointer for UI thumbs only")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (smoke "edit/asset-upsert creates kind=character with appearance and ref_hashes")
    (smoke "shot-cast and shot-ref bind assets to a shot; which-shots-use-asset is a rows query")
    (smoke "edit/expand returns action prompt + cast appearances + unioned ref hashes")
    (smoke "edit/promote-take mints an asset from a landed take's media_hash")
    (smoke "asset.media_id optional; gen path still uses primary_hash on disk")
    (smoke "no shot.refs JSON column — cast/ref are typed rows")
    (details
     (doc (|
        Package API sketch (prefix edit/):
          (edit/asset-upsert project slug kind summary bible
                             appearance negative primary_hash
                             primary_path ref_hashes voice_ref
                             lora_ref media_id meta)
          (edit/asset-get slug) / (edit/assets ?project ?kind)
          (edit/shot-cast shot-id asset-id role [wardrobe-id])
          (edit/shot-cast-clear shot-id)
          (edit/shot-ref shot-id asset-id purpose [weight])
          (edit/expand shot-id) → structured bundle (pure over rows)
          (edit/promote-take take-id kind slug) → asset id

        Character lock workflow (human or agent):
          1. upsert kind=character with bible + appearance text
          2. generate or upload sheet/turnaround → take or media put
          3. set primary_hash/ref_hashes (and optional media_id thumb)
          4. bind via shot-cast; never restate full appearance in
             shot.prompt — prompt stays action/dialogue
          5. on continuity failure: edit asset or wardrobe, re-gen
             only shots that bind that asset

        Voice: kind=voice asset holds sample hash + voice_ref for
        the TTS backend; character.voice_ref points at it; EDL
        audio field still references the dialogue take id.

        Studio-wide vs project: project="" means available to all
        projects on the branch (personal garden pack). Project-
        scoped assets win on slug clash only if callers pass
        project explicitly — default lookup: project then studio.
     |))))

  )
  (subsection sound
   (doc "Production audio as data: line rows (dialogue with chosen takes), cue rows (beds + spot hits on the timeline), mix-job bounce keyed on cue_hash; dailies proxy with sound — no Blender dependency")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (smoke "line rows bind text to character and shot; line.take chooses an audio take")
    (smoke "audio takes are ordinary take rows (backend tts-* or mic) on the shot")
    (smoke "cue rows place beds and hits on the timeline in frames with gain_db")
    (smoke "export writes a cue sheet + ffmpeg bounce script on disk for a cut (v1)")
    (smoke "edit/dailies bounces chosen picture + lines + cues to a proxy mp4 with audio (v2)")
    (smoke "mix-job on unchanged audio graph returns cache hit; changing a line take or cue changes cue_hash")
    (details
     (doc (|
        Package API sketch (prefix edit/):
          (edit/line-upsert project shot ord character text notes)
          (edit/lines shot-id)
          (edit/line-choose line-id take-id)
          (edit/cue-add cut mode asset tc-start dur gain-db [loop])
          (edit/cues cut)
          (edit/dailies cut)      ; ffmpeg proxy bounce, fast preset
          (edit/mix cut profile)  ; stem + full bounce, memoized

        Presets ship with the package (code, not rows):
          dailies — normalize dialogue, beds -20 dB, light limiter,
                    fast encode; seconds of wall time per minute
          flat    — unity gain stems for handing to a human DAW
          film    — dialogue forward, gentle bus compression

        v1 sync, v2 jobs: local TTS and mic ingest are synchronous
        in v1 (no new prims); premium TTS batches join the v2 job
        queue with the same take semantics as picture gen.

        The beginner ADR loop, plainly:
          a line reads wrong → record or generate a new take →
          edit/line-choose the new id → the next bounce uses it.
          The old read stays in the take list; nothing is destroyed.
     |))))
  )
